//? Title: Prospective power for t tests and one-way ANOVA
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Find integer sample sizes for t tests

The one-sample effect uses the population SD. The paired effect uses
the SD of the within-pair differences, and the independent-sample
effect uses the groups' common population SD. All three designs use a
5% level and target 80% power. For each design, the reported count is
the smallest admissible integer count that reaches the target.

```{cpp, id=t-test-designs, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/power.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const auto one = one_sample_t_test_sample_size(.5, .8);
    const auto pairs = paired_t_test_sample_size(-.5, .8, .05, Alternative::Less);
    const auto [first, second] = pooled_t_test_sample_size(.6, .8, {2, 3});
    std::println("| Design | Rejection region | Standardized effect | Size | Power |");
    std::println("| --- | --- | ---: | --- | ---: |");
    std::println("| One sample | Both tails | 0.5 | {} observations | {:.3f} |",
                 one, one_sample_t_test_power(one, .5));
    std::println("| Paired | Lower tail | -0.5 | {} pairs | {:.3f} |",
                 pairs, paired_t_test_power(pairs, -.5, .05, Alternative::Less));
    std::println("| Independent with pooled SD | Both tails | 0.6 | {} and {} observations | {:.3f} |",
                 first, second, pooled_t_test_power(first, second, .6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Design | Rejection region | Standardized effect | Size | Power |
| --- | --- | ---: | --- | ---: |
| One sample | Both tails | 0.5 | 34 observations | 0.808 |
| Paired | Lower tail | -0.5 | 27 pairs | 0.812 |
| Independent with pooled SD | Both tails | 0.6 | 38 and 57 observations | 0.809 |
// result_end

> Compare allocations for one-way ANOVA

The group means are 10, 11, and 14, with a common variance of 16.
The two allocations both use 55 observations. Each allocation weights
the group means differently when computing the alternative
noncentrality, even though the degrees of freedom stay the same.

```{cpp, id=one-way-anova-designs, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/power.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using datamunge::stats::one_way_anova_power;
    const std::vector<double> means{10, 11, 14};
    std::println("| Group sizes | Power at the 5% level |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: |");
    std::println("| 18, 18, 19 | {:.3f} |", one_way_anova_power(means, {18, 18, 19}, 16));
    std::println("| 10, 18, 27 | {:.3f} |", one_way_anova_power(means, {10, 18, 27}, 16));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Group sizes | Power at the 5% level |
| --- | ---: |
| 18, 18, 19 | 0.795 |
| 10, 18, 27 | 0.795 |
// result_end

> Plan an unequal allocation for two proportions

The null risks are 0.35 and 0.25, so the null difference is already
0.10. Under the alternative the first risk rises to 0.50 and the second
stays at 0.25. The normal approximation uses the supplied null risks for
the rejection threshold and the alternative risks for power.

```{cpp, id=two-proportion-allocation, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/power.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::pair null_risks{.35, .25}, alternative_risks{.50, .25};
    const auto [first, second] = two_proportion_sample_size(null_risks, alternative_risks, .8, {2, 3});
    std::println("| First group | Second group | Two-sided power |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} |", first - 2, second - 3,
                 two_proportion_power(first - 2, second - 3, null_risks, alternative_risks));
    std::println("| {} | {} | {:.3f} |", first, second,
                 two_proportion_power(first, second, null_risks, alternative_risks));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| First group | Second group | Two-sided power |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 124 | 186 | 0.796 |
| 126 | 189 | 0.802 |
// result_end

> Compare proportional-hazards event requirements

For a target hazard ratio of 0.75, the Schoenfeld formula uses the log
hazard ratio and the allocation fraction to estimate the required
observed events. Both calculations target 80% power at a two-sided
5% level. Converting events to enrollment would also require assumptions
about event incidence and follow-up.

```{cpp, id=schoenfeld-event-requirements, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/power.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using datamunge::stats::schoenfeld_event_count;
    std::println("| First-group allocation | Required observed events |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto fraction : {.5, .4}) {
        std::println("| {:.0f}% | {} |", fraction * 100, schoenfeld_event_count(.75, fraction, .8));
    }
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| First-group allocation | Required observed events |
| ---: | ---: |
| 50% | 380 |
| 40% | 396 |
// result_end

> Compare cluster size patterns at fixed enrollment

Each arm has six clusters and 120 individuals. The second arm stays
fixed. The first arm uses either six clusters of size 20 or a mixture
of smaller and larger clusters. At the same intracluster correlation,
the larger clusters account for more pairs of correlated observations.
The effective sizes below are the independent sample sizes that would
give the same variances for the individual-weighted means.

```{cpp, id=unequal-cluster-power, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/stats/power.hpp>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge::stats;
    const std::vector<std::size_t> second{18, 20, 22, 18, 20, 22};
    constexpr double rho = .05;
    std::println("| First arm | First-arm design effect | First-arm effective size | Mean power | Proportion power |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    const auto show = [&](const char* label, const std::vector<std::size_t>& first) {
        const auto means = cluster_randomized_mean_power(first, second, rho, .5);
        const auto proportions = cluster_randomized_proportion_power(first, second, rho, {.35, .25}, {.50, .25});
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.1f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", label, means.first.design_effect,
                     means.first.effective_sample_size, means.power, proportions.power);
    };
    show("Six equal clusters", {20, 20, 20, 20, 20, 20});
    show("Sizes 5, 10, 15, 25, 30, 35", {5, 10, 15, 25, 30, 35});
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| First arm | First-arm design effect | First-arm effective size | Mean power | Proportion power |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Six equal clusters | 1.950 | 61.5 | 0.791 | 0.448 |
| Sizes 5, 10, 15, 25, 30, 35 | 2.242 | 53.5 | 0.762 | 0.421 |
// result_end

Mean power uses a standardized difference of 0.5. Proportion power uses
the null and alternative risks from the two-proportion example.

